## Skipping Ahead with `continue`

`break` exits the loop entirely. `continue` just skips the rest of THIS iteration and moves to the next one.

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  var i = 0;
  while i < 5 {
    i = i + 1;
    if i == 3 {
      continue;  // Skip printing when i is 3
    }
    io.println("Processing...");
  }
  return 0;
}
```

This prints for i=1, 2, 4, 5 — but skips 3.

### Break vs Continue

```xiom
// break: "I'm done — exit the loop"
while condition {
  if done {
    break;    // Exit loop completely
  }
}

// continue: "Skip this one — next please"
while condition {
  if skip_this {
    continue; // Jump to next iteration
  }
}
```

### Practical Uses

```xiom
// Process only even numbers
var i = 0;
while i < 20 {
  i = i + 1;
  if i % 2 != 0 {
    continue;  // Skip odd numbers
  }
  io.println("Even number processed!");
}

// Skip invalid data
var i = 0;
while i < 10 {
  i = i + 1;
  if i < 0 {
    continue;  // Skip negative values
  }
  io.println("Valid!");
}
```

### Continue is Like a Filter

Think of `continue` as a filter that says "not this one" — the loop keeps going, just without processing the current value:

```xiom
while condition {
  if not_interested {
    continue;  // "Nope, next!"
  }
  // This only runs for values we ARE interested in
  do_work();
}
```

### Common Pattern: Guard at the Top

```xiom
while i < 100 {
  if should_skip(i) {
    i = i + 1;     // Don't forget to increment!
    continue;
  }
  process(i);
  i = i + 1;
}
```

Be careful: if `continue` skips your increment statement, you get an infinite loop!

